WEBVTT

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.630
And it’s also studded with calcium channels that are linked to voltage-sensitive proteins

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.170
in the membrane of the muscle cell.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.430
Say I want to move my arm.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:12.730
My brain sends an action potential along the motor neuron until it synapses with a muscle cell in my arm.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.610
The receptors on that muscle cell are ligand-gated sodium channels, so when the motor neuron

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.010
releases our old friend acetylcholine into the synapse, the channels open up, and create

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.310
a rush of sodium into the cell as a graded potential, which, if it’s strong enough,

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.290
causes nearby voltage-gated sodium channels to open.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.870
Now, I want to take a second and point out here that we’re still talking about an action

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.830
potential, but not in a neuron. This is happening in a muscle cell, people.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.410
So that action potential zips along a muscle cell’s membrane, the sarcolemma, which has

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.390
lots of tubes that run deep inside the cell, called T-Tubules.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.310
When the action potential travels down one of those tubes, it eventually triggers the

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.070
voltage-sensitive proteins that are linked to those calcium channels on the cell’s sarcoplasmic reticulum.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.270
When those channels are thrown open, the calcium stored inside rushes into the rest of the

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.470
cell, and finally myosin is like, YES! Here we go!

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.750
At this point, the myosin is totally stoked, because the bodyguards that have been frustrating

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.590
it are in for a big, irresistible distraction.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.870
That’s because the protein troponin just loves to bind with calcium, and remember:

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:15.870
When stuff binds to proteins, the proteins change shape.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.510
So the calcium latches on to the troponin and causes it to pull the other bodyguard

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.810
protein -- the tropomyosin -- away from the sites on the actin strands that the myosin

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.310
really wants to get its paws on.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.230
And suddenly it’s all, “Okay?” “Okay.”
